description.narrative:
In consultation with social partners and other stakeholders, the Government of Kenya, enacted the Children Act 2022 and assented into law on 6 July 2022. The Act took effect on 26 July 2022. It aligns to the international labour standards and the Constitution of Kenya, with provisions of key ILO standards on Child Labour, Forced Labour and Trafficking in Persons.  Additionally, in  June 2022, the Government of Kenya, in consultation with social partners, revised the child labour policy.         
The revised policy is anchored under the main goal “to realize the eradication of child labour especially its worst forms in Kenya” and is anchored under 6 key policy objectives:
1) To promote decent work for adults and youth above minimum working age;
2) To end child labour in agriculture and other priority sectors;
3) To accelerate the realization to children’s right to education and training;
4) To scale up access to social protection for families affected by child labour;
5) To strengthen child labour knowledge and data management, for evidence based policy and programmatic response and for undertaking  effective monitoring and evaluation; and
6) To leverage partnerships and alliances for financing the policy. In the review of the 2022 Children's Act, ILO, with support from the United States Department of Labor (USDOL), facilitated convening of technical working groups and also provided technical support on child labour. It also provided support to the whole process of review of the Child labour policy. The ILO supported the Government in June 2022 to revise the national policy on elimination of child labour to succeed the National Policy on Elimination of Child Labour of 2016. It supported convening technical working committee meetings – June 2022 and May 2023 - with the aim of providing technical insights for the review of the draft Policy document which the final draft has been shared for comments from the National Technical Committee on elimination of child labour.
